            </HouseholdOperatingConditions>
            <WaterConservation LowFlushToilets="2" LowFlushToiletsUpgrade="0" />
            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

&lt;b&gt;Observed Air Leakage:&lt;/b&gt;

The house was tested to have an air leakage of 4.5 air changes per hour (ACH) at 50 pascals of pressure. This isn't too bad for a house of this age but it's not going to hurt to do a little bit of air sealing.



Electrical outlets/switches along outside walls had some air leakage. This can be helped by spray foaming around the outside of the electrical boxes, if it's accessable, as well as placing foam gaskets behind the outlet plates.



Anywhere there is a wire or a pipe exiting the exterior wall usually has a small gap and is a location where air leakage can occur. Some examples are the wires in the electrical box and where the oil pipes enter the basement.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

For a minimum 10% improvement you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 120&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;4 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 500 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must have a home energy evaluation, which includes an air leakage test, before and after your retrofits. For a full rebate, the rebate application, with a copy of the first page of both home energy evaluation reports, must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of the second energy evaluation. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

The exterior walls of your house appear to be 2x4 studs with 4 inches (R12) of fiberglass batt insulation. I have modelled adding 3 inches (R15) of semi-rigid, breathable exterior insulation. This project would include adding insulation to the pony wall of the basement since the foam insulation would just continue down to the foundation.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By adding R15 of semi-rigid, breathable exterior insulation, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 1025&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;34 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 1,875 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 0.7 (R-3.8). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website:

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------</mainWalls>
                <ceilings>

The attic of your house has 3 inches of blown-in cellulose insulation. I have modelled adding 13 inches (R44) blown-in cellulose insulation which would bring your attic up to about R50. You would have to build up the framing around the attic hatch so the insulation won't fall out. 



The attic hatch itself should be well insulated as well, this can be done by glueing at least 4 inches of styrofoam insulation to the top of the hatch.



The attic hatch has some weatherstripping around the edge and had a pretty good seal when the air leakage test was done. You may need to replace this weatherstripping if it is beginning to wear out.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By adding 13 inches (R44) blown-in cellulose insulation to the attic, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 870&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;29 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 750 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total ceiling area and achieve a minimum insulation value of RSI 7.0 (R-40). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of ceiling area that is insulated compared to the total ceiling area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

</ceilings>
                <foundation>

Your basement walls appear to be 8 inches of concrete with about 2 inches of fiberglass batt insulation. I have modeled adding 2 inches (R10) rigid foam, extruded polystyrene insulation. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By adding 2 inches (R10) rigid foam extruded polystyrene insulation to the basement walls you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 205&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;7 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 1,250 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total foundation wall area including crawlspaces (when applicable), and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.8 (R-10). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

</foundation>
                <windows>

As all of your windows are older double pane ones that do not appear to have a Low E coating or an insulating gas fill between the panes of glass, I have modeled the potential energy savings associated with upgrading all of them with ENERGY STARÂ® qualified windows rated for climate zone 3. In addition, I recommend triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt; 

By replacing all windows with triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged, ENERGY STARÂ® qualified windows rated for climate zone 3, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 345&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;12 GJ&lt;/b&gt;. There are currently no rebates available for installing new windows from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

</windows>
                <VentilationSystem>

I did not see a combustion air supply pipe to provide combustion air to the boiler and hot water tank.

I suggest you have a qualified heating contractor investigate this further and ensure both the fresh air supply requirements of your house and the combustion air supply needs of your boiler and hot water tank are satisfied. The combustion air pipe should have an electric damper installed to ensure combustion air will only be introduced into the room when the boiler or hot water tank is operating.



---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

</VentilationSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

There is an open combustion air duct in the mechanical room which is required for the furnace and hot water tank to operate. The combustion air pipe should have an electric damper installed to ensure combustion air will only be introduced into the room when either the furnace or hot water tank are operating.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By adding an electronic damper to the combustion air duct, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 180&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;6 GJ&lt;/b&gt;.



--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------</HeatingSystem>
                <AdditionalComments>

Electronically Commutated Motors (ECM) save pumping energy for circulation pumps and hydronic heating systems, such as baseboard convectors and in floor radiant by at least 50% and usually much more compared with other pumps. Typical pumps use a constant amount of power regardless of how much water needs to be circulated throughout the building, and are often significantly oversized. By contrast an ECM pump senses how hard it has to work to circulate the heating fluid and adjusts the flow rate accordingly. In addition, by reducing the flow, they can also increase the temperature difference between water leaving the boiler and the water returning which also saves heating fuel by increasing boiler efficiency.  These pumps determine the minimal output required to achieve optimal comfort. 



Some ECM circulators use an integrated logic board to ÂlearnÂ the systemÂs usage patterns in order to boost the standard 50 percent energy savings into the 70-80 percent range. As the valves in the different sections open or close with rising or falling demand, the pumps automatically ramp up or down to meet the load. Even though these pumps are typically 2-3 times more expensive, with the huge savings potential, their payback is typically very quick.  When you purchase and install an ECM pump in your house you can qualify for &lt;b&gt;$ 175 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



ADDITIONAL COMMENTS



&lt;b&gt;Fuel Costs - Yellowknife&lt;/b&gt;

The following values have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Yellowknife:

Electricity - $19.45 min, $0.30 per kWh

Oil  - $0.84 per litre 

Propane - $0.71 per litre

Wood - $341 per cord 

Pellets - $6.58 per bag



---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



